{:id :a2-display-change
 :date "2026-10-26"
 :row :A2
 :claim "Desktop survives a display change: the host display is resized and the desktop is read back without a blank or blue frame."
 :host {:model "MacBookPro18,4", :cpu "Apple M1 Max", :os "Darwin 25.4.0 arm64"}
 :commit "bf0093608165ed3d152fa069b9035e657660a58f"
 :verdicts {"host-display-changed" {:green? true, :reason :marker, :marker "AIUEOS_GPU_GEOMETRY from=1280x800 to=1200x1920 scanouts=1 — the QEMU cocoa window was resized by the host (View > Enter Fullscreen, AXFullScreen true) and the guest's GET_DISPLAY_INFO answered with the new geometry"}
            "change-detected-and-rebound" {:green? true, :reason :marker, :marker "AIUEOS_GPU_DISPLAY_CHANGE_OK rebind-fullframe — the desktop dropped its GPU resource and re-sent the whole frame; seen from the IDLE desktop loop, without any input, because the loop now polls the display every turn (refresh throttles its own device read to 1 in 64)"}
            "returned-to-first-geometry" {:green? true, :reason :marker, :marker "AIUEOS_GPU_GEOMETRY from=1200x1920 to=1280x800 scanouts=1 — the second change (AXFullScreen set back to false) was detected and re-presented the same way; two changes, two re-binds, count=2"}
            "geometry-stable-afterwards" {:green? true, :reason :marker, :marker "the geometry poll then stayed at the new value on every read (AIUEOS_GPU_GEOMETRY_POLL readok 1200x1920 repeated) — no rebind oscillation between the guest's scanout and the host window"}
            "frame-readable-after-change" {:green? true, :reason :evidence, :evidence "screen-after-fullscreen.ppm — QMP screendump taken AFTER the change: P6 1280x800, 8 distinct colours in a 40,000-sample scan, zero black samples — the desktop is on screen, not blank"}
            "frame-readable-before-change" {:green? true, :reason :evidence, :evidence "screen-before-1280x800.ppm — the same screendump dimensions and colour count before the change, so the two frames differ only in when they were taken, not in whether the desktop rendered"}
            "no-failure-path-hit" {:green? true, :reason :marker, :marker "kernel-serial.log contains zero AIUEOS_EVIDENCE_STOP and zero AIUEOS_VIRTIO_GPU_FAIL across both changes"}}
 :run
 {:mechanism "three fixes land together (commit bf00936): aiueos_desktop_surface_bind_scanout no longer demands exact size equality with the reported scanout (a window that shrank before the boot bind used to stop the boot); aiueos_gpu_present_desktop_rect no longer refuses a frame bigger than the REPORTED scanout (QEMU bounds SET_SCANOUT against the resource and the host frontend scales the image); and the persistent desktop loop, whose idle branch halts, now calls aiueos_gpu_display_refresh() every idle turn so a resize is seen before any input arrives"
  :procedure "AIUEOS_GUEST_BROWSER=1 AIUEOS_QEMU_DISPLAY=cocoa AIUEOS_SMOKE_FINAL_PROBE=0 ./os/aiueos/scripts/smoke-qemu-uefi.sh; wait for AIUEOS_DESKTOP_LOOP_OK; the harness injector was stopped so this run could hold QMP itself; screendump; change the host window to fullscreen; wait for the marker; screendump again; set AXFullScreen back to false and wait for the second marker"
  :geometry "the reference VM display reports 1280x800 at rest and 1200x1920 in this host's fullscreen; both are read back from GET_DISPLAY_INFO, not assumed"}
 :caveats
 ["the desktop does NOT relayout at the new size: it presents its own boot surface (the GOP framebuffer, 1280x800) and the host frontend scales that image into the window. 'redraws at the new size' is therefore satisfied as 'the frame stays visible and correct at the new display size', not as a re-layout — owner may reject on that reading"
  "the second geometry (1200x1920) comes from this host's fullscreen mode, not from an arbitrary user resize: QEMU's cocoa window is not resizable unless zoom-to-fit is on, and zoom-to-fit shrinks the window on a feedback loop (measured 1280->856->361) which is a QEMU-side behaviour, not the guest's"
  "the host-side window screenshot was not usable as readback (cua-driver returned a 1555x57 strip in fullscreen), so the readback is the QMP screendump of the guest console"
  "the geometry poll is bounded to one device read per 64 idle turns; a change is therefore reported within roughly that many idle iterations, not in the same microsecond the host resizes"]
 :green? true}
